    <!-- Unit Overview -->
    <section class="welcome-section">
        <h2><i class="fa-solid fa-compass section-icon"></i> Unit Overview</h2>
        <p>of Computer Science Unit Foundation Concepts The focus is on Scalability and Reliability in non-traditional environments. Students move from simple table storage to high-availability systems. Key themes include the ACID vs. BASE trade-off, Consistency in Distributed Systems, and the extraction of value from "Big Data" through OLAP and Data Mining.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- Syllabus Links -->
    <section class="content-section">
        <h2><i class="fa-solid fa-bookmark section-icon"></i> What You'll Learn</h2>
        <div class="learning-points">
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <strong>A3.3.4</strong> — -5: Advanced Transaction Management: The ACID properties (Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, Durability) and Transaction Control Language (COMMIT, ROLLBACK).
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>A3.3.6</strong> — Database Views: Benefits for security, simplicity, and performance.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>A3.4.1</strong> — Alternative Models: NoSQL (Document, Key-Value, Graph, Columnar) vs. Relational. Cloud and Spatial databases.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>A3.4.1</strong> — Distributed Databases: Features, fragmentation, replication, and the challenges of consistency.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>A3.4.2</strong> — -3: Data Warehousing: ETL process, Data Mining techniques (Cluster, Classify, Associate), and OLAP (Online Analytical Processing) for Business Intelligence.
                </li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </section>

