    <!-- Watch Out For -->
    <section class="exam-section" style="background: linear-gradient(135deg, #fff3cd 0%, #ffe5a0 100%); border-left-color: #ffc107;">
        <h2><i class="fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation section-icon" style="color: #856404;"></i> Common Misconceptions</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>NoSQL vs. SQL:</strong> Believing NoSQL is "better." It is a trade-off: NoSQL offers scaling and flexibility but usually sacrifices strict ACID compliance.</li>
            <li><strong>Distributed Consistency:</strong> Thinking data is updated everywhere instantly; students must understand "Eventual Consistency."</li>
            <li><strong>Data Mining:</strong> Confusing it with a standard search. Mining is about discovering unknown patterns, not finding specific records.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- Big Ideas -->
    <section class="content-section" style="border-top-color: var(--accent-color);">
        <h2><i class="fa-solid fa-lightbulb section-icon"></i> Big Ideas to Consider</h2>
        <div class="learning-points">
            <ul>
                <li><strong>The Value of Patterns:</strong> In Data Mining, we find correlations. Does a correlation between two data points constitute "knowledge" or just a statistical coincidence?</li>
                <li><strong>Privacy vs. Intelligence:</strong> Does a company's "Business Intelligence" (OLAP) infringe on a consumer's right to remain un-categorised?</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </section>

